("Trebuchet",-400,"Traction trebuchet 4th century BCE; counterweight 12th century","China; Middle East","Chinese and Islamic engineers","Breached castle walls","A siege engine using a long lever arm and counterweight.",
 "The traction trebuchet, pulled by teams of people, appeared in China in the Warring States period. Muslim and Byzantine engineers developed the counterweight trebuchet in the 12th century.","Edward I's 'Warwolf' at Stirling (1304) was so prized that he refused the surrender until it was tested. Mongols reportedly threw plague corpses into Caffa (1346).","It made castle walls taller and thicker until gunpowder made walls obsolete.","Mongol conquests; Black Death"),
("Stirrup",300,"c. 4th century CE","China (or Central Asian steppe)","Unknown","Mounted warfare","A foot support for riders.",
 "Single mounting stirrups appear in India (2nd century BCE toe loops) and full paired stirrups in China around 322 CE. They spread to Europe via the Avars.","Historian Lynn White argued stirrups created feudal knights; many historians consider this too simple.","Stirrups gave riders stability for shock charges.","Mongol conquests"),
("Longbow",1250,"Medieval","England and Wales","Welsh and English archers","Won Crécy and Agincourt","A tall yew bow with a draw weight of 70–80 kg.",
 "English law required men to practise archery on Sundays.","Skeletons from the Mary Rose show archers' deformed shoulders from years of training.","Ended the dominance of armoured knights.","Battle of Agincourt; Hundred Years' War"),
("Machine gun",1884,"1884","Britain/USA","Hiram Maxim","Mass killing in colonial wars and WWI","A gun firing automatically using recoil energy.",
 "Hiram Maxim's gun fired 600 rounds per minute.","Hilaire Belloc wrote: 'Whatever happens, we have got the Maxim gun, and they have not.' At Omdurman (1898) British forces killed about 10,000 Sudanese for fewer than 50 losses.","It locked WWI into trench stalemate.","Scramble for Africa; World War I; Battle of the Somme"),
("Poison gas",1915,"1915","Germany","Fritz Haber","Chemical warfare","Chlorine, phosgene and mustard gas.",
 "Fritz Haber, who also invented fertiliser synthesis that feeds billions, personally oversaw the first chlorine gas attack at Ypres in 1915.","His wife Clara Immerwahr, a chemist, killed herself soon after, reportedly in protest. Haber's institute later developed Zyklon, used by the Nazis in gas chambers.","Banned by the 1925 Geneva Protocol, but used again in Ethiopia, Iraq and Syria.","World War I; The Holocaust"),
("Tank",1916,"1916","Britain","British engineers (Landships Committee)","Broke trench deadlock","An armoured tracked vehicle.",
 "Called 'tanks' to keep them secret, as if they were water carriers; first used at the Somme in September 1916.","Early tanks broke down often.","They transformed warfare in WWII (blitzkrieg).","Battle of the Somme; World War I"),
