("Age of castles",900,1500,"c. 900–1500","Europe and Middle East","Lords ruled from fortified castles, with peasants working the land around them; sieges were the most common form of war, lasting months.","Bread, pottage, ale; stored grain for sieges.","—","After Viking raids and feudal fragmentation, lords built thousands of castles across Europe: wooden motte-and-baileys after 1066, then stone keeps and concentric castles inspired by Crusader fortresses. Gunpowder artillery ended their military role.","Castle"),
("Gunpowder empires",1450,1750,"c. 1450–1750","Ottoman, Safavid and Mughal empires","Large armies of musketeers and artillery; huge cities like Istanbul, Isfahan and Agra.","Varied regional cuisines; coffee spread.","—","Historians Marshall Hodgson and William McNeill named the Ottoman, Safavid and Mughal empires 'gunpowder empires', because cannon and muskets let rulers smash rival fortresses and centralise power.","Gunpowder_empires"),
("Age of Revolutions",1765,1848,"c. 1765–1848","Atlantic world","Ideas of liberty, rights and nationhood spread; peasants and slaves rose up.","—","—","The American, French, Haitian and Latin American revolutions overthrew monarchies and colonial rule, spreading ideas of popular sovereignty.","Age_of_Revolution"),
